COMMAND NAME: mpp_restore

Restores Bizgres MPP databases that were backed up using mpp_dumpall.

This script is located in $MPPHOME/bin

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mpp_restore -mpp-k=<timestamp_key> -d <database_name> [-h <hostname>] 
[-p port] [-U <username>] [-W] [-a] [-i] [-o] [-O] [-s] [-t <table_name>] 
[-x] [--mpp-e] [--mpp-d=<directoryname>] [--mpp-r=<filename>]

*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mpp_restore utility recreates the data definitions (schema) and user 
data in a Bizgres MPP database using the script files created by an 
mpp_dumpall operation. The use of this script assumes:

1. You have backup files created by an mpp_dumpall operation.
2. Your Bizgres MPP system up and running.
3. Your Bizgres MPP system has the exact same number of segment instances 
   (primary and mirror) as the system that was backed up using mpp_dumpall. 
4. (optional) The mpp_restore script uses the information in the MPP system 
   catalog tables to determine the hosts, ports, and data directories for the 
   segment instances it is restoring. If you want to change any of this 
   information (for example, move the system to a different array of hosts) 
   you must use the mpprebuildcluster.sh and mpprebuildseg.sh scripts to 
   reconfigure your array before restoring.
5. The databases you are restoring have been created in the system.

The functionality of mpp_restore is analogous to PostgreSQLs pg_restore 
utility, which restores a database from files created by the database backup 
process. It issues the commands necessary to reconstruct the database to the 
state it was in at the time it was saved.

The functionality of mpp_restore is modified to accommodate the distributed 
nature of a Bizgres MPP database, and to use files created by an mpp_dumpall 
operation. Keep in mind that a database in Bizgres MPP is actually comprised 
of several PostgreSQL instances (the master and all segments), each of which 
must be restored individually. The mpp_restore utility takes care of 
populating each segment in the system with its own distinct portion of data.

The mpp_restore utility performs the following actions:

ON THE MASTER HOST

* Creates the user database schema(s) using the 
  mpp_dump_<master_id>_<timestamp> SQL file created by mpp_dumpall.
* Creates a log file in the master data directory named 
  mpp_restore_status_<master_id>_<timestamp>.
* mpp_restore launches a mpp_restore_agent for each segment instance to be 
  restored. mpp_restore_agent processes run on the segment hosts and report 
  status back to the mpp_restore process running on the master host. 

ON THE SEGMENT HOSTS

* Restores the user data for each segment instance using the 
  mpp_dump_<seg_id>_<timestamp> files created by mpp_dumpall. Each segment 
  instance on a host (primary and mirror instances) are restored.
* Creates a log file for each segment instance named 
  mpp_restore_status_<seg_id>_<timestamp>.

Note that the 14 digit timestamp is the number that uniquely identifies the 
backup job to be restored, and is part of the filename for each dump file 
created by a mpp_dumpall operation. This timestamp must be passed to the 
mpp_restore utility when restoring a Bizgres MPP database. 


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-mpp-k=<timestamp_key>

Required. The 14 digit timestamp key that uniquely identifies the backup 
set of data to restore. This timestamp can be found in the mpp_dumpall 
log file output, as well as at the end of the dump files created by a 
mpp_dumpall operation. It is of the form YYYYMMDDHHMMSS.

-d <database_name>

Required. The name of the database to connect to in order to restore 
the user data. The database(s) you are restoring must exist, mpp_restore 
does not create the database. If restoring multiple databases, connect 
to template1.

-h <hostname>
The host name of the MPP master host. If not provided, the value of $PGHOST 
or the local host is used. This option can also be specified as --host=hostname.

-p <port>

The MPP master port. If not provided, the value of $PGPORT or the port 
number provided at compile time is used. This option can also be specified 
as --port=port.

-U <username>
The database super user account name, for example bgadmin. If not provided, 
the value of $PGUSER or the current OS user name is used. This option can 
also be specified as --username.

-W
Forces a password prompt. This will happen automatically if the server 
requires password authentication.

-a

Restore only the data, not the schema (data definitions). This option can 
also be specified as --data-only.

-i
Ignores a version mismatch between mpp_restore and the database server. 
This option can also be specified as --ignore-version.

-o

Restore items in order by OID. This option is of limited usefulness, 
since OID is only an approximate indication of original creation order. 
This option can also be specified as --oid-order.

-O

Do not run commands to set ownership of objects to match the original 
database. By default, mpp_restore issues SET SESSION AUTHORIZATION 
statements to set ownership of created schema elements. This option 
can also be specified as --no-owner.

-s

Restores only the schema (data definitions), no user data is restored. 
This option can also be specified as --schema-only.

-t <table_name>

Restores the table definition and/or table data for the specified 
table only. This option can also be specified as --table=table. 

-x
Prevents the restoration of access privileges (GRANT/REVOKE commands).
This option can also be specified as --no-privileges or no-acl.

--mpp-e
Specifies that processing should ignore any errors that occur. Use this 
option to continue restore processing on errors.

--mpp-d=<directoryname>

Specifies the relative or absolute path to backup files on the hosts. 
If this is a relative path, it is considered to be relative to the data 
directory. If not specified, defaults to the data directory of each i
nstance being restored. Use this option if you created your backup files 
in an alternate location when running mpp_dumpall.

--mpp-r=<filename>

Specifies the full path name where the restore job log files will be placed 
on each host. If not specified, defaults to the data directory of each 
instance being restored. Using this option may be desirable if each segment 
host has multiple segment instances  it will create the log files in a 
centralized location.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Restore all schema and user data for an MPP system using backup files 
created by mpp_dumpall:

mpp_restore -mpp-k=2005103112453 -d template1


